<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.Verify</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>harness-verify</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    harness-verify — DB-6 ("ISOLATION BY CONSTRUCTION") EXERCISED AGAINST A LIVE DEVICE. READ-ONLY.

    WHY IT EXISTS. `MirrorClient` is constructed in exactly ONE production place — Harness.Loop's
    LoopRun.Execute, and no executable reaches that line: `harness-run` with the verify flag stops at the
    VerifyingDeviceGateway, which reads the version registers through the raw transport and never builds
    a client. So `VersionCheck.Confirm` and `MirrorClient.ReadControl`'s DB-6 guard had never executed
    against a real device. Both were unit-tested against scripted transports, which is a different claim.

    *** THIS BINARY READS AND NOTHING ELSE, AND THE CLAIM IS CHECKED RATHER THAN DECLARED. ***
    VerifyStructureTests walks every method body in the compiled assembly and asserts that none of them
    names a write member of Harness.Wire, Harness.Map or NModbus — with a DENOMINATOR (bodies examined
    > 0), a LIVE POSITIVE CONTROL (a planted class in the test assembly that does call
    WriteHoldingRegisters, which the same walk must find) and a RESOLUTION CONTROL inside this module
    (MirrorClient.ReadControl, which VerifyRun certainly calls). A `const bool` with an Assert.False on
    it is what that replaces: measured 2026-08-14 on Harness.RigWrite, planting a class that constructed
    a live socket client left all 202 tests green.

    THE RESIDUAL, NAMED. Unlike harness-mirror-read this binary MUST hold a MirrorClient — exercising
    the client's guard is the whole point — and MirrorClient carries WriteVector/Commit/LowerAllStartBools/
    ClearStartEcho. So the property checked here is the narrower and more precise one: this assembly
    names NONE of those members, and names neither LoopRun.Execute nor LoopCli.Run, the two entry points
    that can reach a write. The capability exists in the process; nothing here reaches it.

    Harness.Run is referenced for LoopCli.Compose and ProgramUnderTest — the SAME loaders and the SAME
    composition `harness-run` in generate-only mode uses. A second parser would be a second answer to the
    question "what is the build stamp for these documents", and the whole tool turns on that number.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Gate\Harness.Gate.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Loop\Harness.Loop.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Run\Harness.Run.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Wire\Harness.Wire.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\device-guard\DeviceGuard\DeviceGuard.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
